1. Ensure logrotate is installed:
   ```bash
   sudo dnf install logrotate -y  # RHEL/CentOS/Fedora
Verify installation:
logrotate --version
Task 1: Edit logrotate.conf for Custom Log Rotation Settings
Subtask 1.1: Examine Default Configuration
View the main configuration file:

sudo cat /etc/logrotate.conf
Expected Output: Shows default rotation settings (weekly, rotate 4, etc.)
Check existing logrotate configurations:

ls /etc/logrotate.d/
Subtask 1.2: Create Custom Configuration
Create a new configuration file for system logs:

sudo vim /etc/logrotate.d/mycustomlogs
Add the following configuration (example for /var/log/syslog):

/var/log/syslog {
    daily
    rotate 7
    compress
    delaycompress
    missingok
    notifempty
    create 0640 root adm
    postrotate
        /usr/bin/systemctl restart rsyslog >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
    endscript
}
Key Parameters:
daily: Rotate logs daily
rotate 7: Keep 7 rotated copies
compress: Use gzip compression
postrotate: Restart rsyslog after rotation
Verify syntax:

sudo logrotate --debug /etc/logrotate.d/mycustomlogs
Troubleshooting: Fix any syntax errors reported
Task 2: Force Log Rotation Using logrotate
Subtask 2.1: Manual Rotation Execution
Force immediate rotation (dry run first):

sudo logrotate --debug --verbose --force /etc/logrotate.d/mycustomlogs
Execute actual rotation:

sudo logrotate --force /etc/logrotate.d/mycustomlogs
Subtask 2.2: Verify Rotation
Check rotated files:
ls -lh /var/log/syslog*
Expected Outcome: Should show original log plus rotated files (e.g., syslog.1.gz)
Task 3: Test and Check Log Rotation Effects
Subtask 3.1: Simulate Log Growth
Generate test log entries:

for i in {1..1000}; do echo "Test log entry $i" | sudo tee -a /var/log/syslog; done
Check log size:

du -sh /var/log/syslog
Subtask 3.2: Trigger and Verify Rotation
Run rotation again:

sudo logrotate --force /etc/logrotate.d/mycustomlogs
Verify new rotation:

ls -lh /var/log/syslog*
Expected Outcome: New rotated file (syslog.1.gz) with original log reset
Check compression:

file /var/log/syslog.1.gz
Should show "gzip compressed data"
